libxtc (1.4.2-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Correctness + API-discipline release, from three carrier-team reports
    and a systematic wrapper audit.
  * Cross-thread wake of an idle loop (lost wakeup): a fiber spawned or a
    mailbox message sent onto a loop parked idle in the io_uring wait
    could be missed.  The io_uring wakeup poll now re-arms before
    draining; audited all eight I/O backends and fixed the identical
    latent bug in the illumos event-ports backend; the level-triggered
    backends (epoll/poll/select), kqueue (EV_CLEAR), and IOCP (posted
    packet) were already safe.  Guarded by a native reproducer and a
    deterministic test_sim_wake_park.
  * Runtime threads are created with all signals blocked
    (__os_pthread_create_masked at every creation site: scheduler, loop,
    worker, blocking pool, deadlock detector, PSI thread), so a runtime
    thread does not inherit a permissive signal mask -- addressing the
    carrier's process-directed SIGCHLD reaching a scheduler thread.  The
    mask primitive is verified correct in isolation; one integration case
    is still under investigation (docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md).
  * Allocation discipline: every internal allocation now goes through the
    __os_* wrappers (allocator hook + preemption bracket + portability),
    fixing several buffers that were raw-malloc'd but handed to callers
    who free them with xtc_free -- a mismatched free / heap corruption
    under a custom allocator (the class of bug an embedder like
    PostgreSQL hits).  __os_malloc/_calloc/_realloc now guarantee a
    non-NULL pointer on XTC_OK.
  * New PUBLIC allocation / time / atomic API so a consumer never needs
    the internal __os_* surface: xtc_malloc, xtc_calloc, xtc_realloc,
    xtc_aligned_alloc, xtc_aligned_free, xtc_clock_mono, xtc_clock_real,
    xtc_sleep_ns, xtc_atomic_i64_load, xtc_atomic_i64_add (complementing
    the existing xtc_free).  All shipped examples were migrated off __os_*
    onto this public surface.
  * New merge/release gate + CI step (test/dist/test_api_discipline.sh)
    enforcing: library uses the __os_* wrappers, __os_ alloc returns are
    checked (never voided, no dead NULL double-check), consumers use only
    xtc_*, and no naked-block-scoped variables.  Rules recorded in
    AGENTS.md.
  * DST: general stale-data (out-of-date read) fault, disk-full ENOSPC,
    clock skew, reboot/incarnation, a write-back crash model that ties
    durability to fsync completion, a determinism-enforcement guard, and
    a bug-injection harness (plant a known safety bug, prove DST catches
    it) -- 46-test sim suite.
  * Known issue documented: xtc_exec_fini can leak a cross-thread-spawned
    proc's task+coro at abnormal teardown (LeakSanitizer; masked on the CI
    runner where LSan is restricted).  See docs/KNOWN_ISSUES.md.
  * Full make check clean under AddressSanitizer and
    UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; amalgamation builds at 1.4.2.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Tue, 07 Jul 2026 16:20:43 -0400

libxtc (1.4.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * DST-metrics + benchmark-fairness release (no public API change).
  * The RIGHT DST yardsticks are now recorded in the steering docs
    (AGENTS.md) and backed by machinery, so libxtc's "as trustworthy as
    FoundationDB / TigerBeetle" goal is measured correctly rather than by
    code-coverage percentage: (1) determinism, 100% and enforced;
    (2) volume survived; (3) fault-space coverage; (4) bug-detection
    latency.
  * Bug-injection harness (yardstick 4): src/inc/xtc_dst_inject.h defines
    planted safety bugs behind -DXTC_DST_INJECT_BUG=<id> (absent in every
    normal build, so production is byte-unchanged), and
    scripts/dst-bug-inject.sh asserts the DST test whose invariant should
    catch each one FAILS.  Three planted bugs -- a dropped mailbox waker,
    a granted-over-conflict lock, and an ack-without-durable-fsync -- are
    all caught (3/3), wired into the sim-dst CI job so every push proves
    DST still catches them.  Building it exposed and documented a real
    DST-coverage gap (the crash tests tie durability to the durable-LSN
    frontier, not to a fsync-confirmed flag).
  * Measured DST line/branch coverage of the public-API-serving source
    and recorded it in docs/M_DST.md with an honest reachability
    breakdown (blocking pool, shared-memory slab, thread rwlock, and
    native AIO backends are covered by other test tiers, not DST).
    test_sim_spawn_rel now exercises xtc_down_decode_ex under DST.
  * M17 conformance fairness: the W1/W3/W7 Tokio drivers no longer
    silently size to all cores.  They read BENCH_WORKERS, and the suite
    runs two symmetric framings -- single-core (per-core efficiency, the
    fair default, and the only meaningful framing for the serial W3) and
    full-parallelism (matched N workers/loops, for W1/W7).
  * Full make check clean under AddressSanitizer and
    UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; 45-test DST sim suite; amalgamation 1.4.1.

