libxtc (1.4.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Feature release, focused on the DST-first goal: make libxtc's
    deterministic simulation as trustworthy as FoundationDB / TigerBeetle
    -- 100% deterministic, mechanically enforced, and adversarial.
  * Determinism ENFORCEMENT (the north star made concrete): a guard traps
    any sim-reachable primitive that would break seed replay (a real wall
    clock, an unseeded RNG, an environment read, a raw thread id) via
    __xtc_sim_nondeterminism(); in strict mode (default) it aborts naming
    the source, and xtc_sim_exec_run refuses to return success if any
    violation was seen -- so every one of the DST tests now PROVES its
    run touched nothing nondeterministic.  A new test proves the guard
    fires and enforces.  Nondeterminism is now impossible to introduce
    silently.
  * FoundationDB-parity fault model, waves 1-3:
    - fault-space COVERAGE tracking (which buggify sites a sweep hit);
    - a semantic end-of-run CONSISTENCY check
      (xtc_sim_set_consistency_check) beyond per-step state hashes -- the
      composition test now scans the whole B-tree for a valid, complete
      structure after the full adversarial gauntlet;
    - asymmetric (one-way) network partition;
    - CLOCK SKEW (xtc_sim_clock_skew): a fiber's observed time disagrees
      with when its timers fire, applied only on the observation seam so
      it never desyncs the scheduler;
    - disk-full ENOSPC write injection (xtc_sim_io_enospc_enable);
    - general STALE-DATA reads (xtc_sim_io_stale_enable): a read returns a
      structurally-valid-but-out-of-date prior version -- a fd+offset
      model reusable by any storage code, with a test proving a version
      check catches it (a bare checksum cannot);
    - REBOOT / incarnation: a rebooted proc's reused pid slot gets a
      distinct generation, so a stale pre-reboot pid is rejected (the
      local analogue of a node incarnation counter).
    The adversarial mode found and fixed a real xtc_lockmgr_destroy
    bad-free during this work.
  * Process API: atomic spawn_link / spawn_monitor (Erlang parity, no
    spawn-then-relate race) and a self-describing DOWN (xtc_down_decode_ex)
    that separates a signal number from an app exit code -- both from
    v1.3.0, reaffirmed here.
  * Portable block-device layer (xtc_bdev): sector-aligned positioned I/O
    over the async xtc_aio path, native geometry probe per OS with a
    regular-file fallback.
  * Design (no code): distributed spawn/link/monitor over network
    connections (docs/M_DISTRIBUTED.md) -- an Erlang-style feature set on
    libxtc's existing substrate, recommended as an optional in-tree
    module, deterministically testable to FDB grade.
  * DST sim suite grows to 45 tests.  Full make check clean under
    AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; qualified on
    riscv64, x86_64, FreeBSD, illumos, Windows 11 ARM64, and musl.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Mon, 06 Jul 2026 14:30:59 -0400

libxtc (1.3.0-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Feature release.
  * Process API -- atomic spawn + link / monitor (Erlang spawn_link /
    spawn_monitor parity): xtc_proc_spawn_link() and
    xtc_proc_spawn_monitor() establish the parent<->child relationship
    BEFORE the child is made runnable, closing the window a
    spawn-then-link/monitor idiom leaves open.  Even a child that runs
    and exits immediately delivers its EXIT or DOWN -- never the
    XTC_DOWN_NOPROC "monitor raced a dead target" outcome.
  * Process API -- self-describing DOWN (requested by the
    multithreaded-PostgreSQL carrier team): the DOWN reason integer
    previously overloaded a signal number (1..255) and a bare app exit
    code from xtc_exit_self(code), so xtc_exit_self(1) was
    indistinguishable from a signal-1 (SIGHUP) contained fault.  A proc
    now records its exit kind and carries it in the DOWN/EXIT envelope;
    the new xtc_down_decode_ex() fills an xtc_down_info_t whose kind
    (XTC_DOWN_KIND_CLEAN / EXIT / SIGNAL / NOPROC) plus separate signal
    and exit_code fields never collide.  The legacy xtc_down_decode()
    is unchanged and still returns the same reason.
  * Deterministic Simulation Testing -- adversarial upgrades toward
    FoundationDB parity: a pessimal scheduler (xtc_sim_sched_pessimal,
    which monopolizes one loop to starve its peers -- the worst
    interleaving), a completion/message swizzle (xtc_sim_swizzle_enable,
    a legal reorder of the io_sim event queue), seed-varied fault
    magnitudes in the swarm, and Buggify planted in the storage/lock
    paths that were bare (wal.flush.tiny_batch, btree.split.eager,
    lock.grant.skip_head).  The adversarial mode found and fixed a real
    latent bug: xtc_lockmgr_destroy freed slab-backed lock entries with
    the OS allocator (a bad-free reachable only with a lock still held
    at destroy).  DST sim suite grows to 43 tests.
  * Concurrency fix -- a cross-thread xtc_send ORed a wake flag into the
    target task's wake_revents from a foreign thread while the owning
    loop also touched it: a non-atomic RMW data race (the only known
    open race in the runtime).  wake_revents is now atomic; verified
    with ThreadSanitizer reporting zero warnings.
  * Windows -- the test_proc::selective_receive regression is resolved
    by the round-2 IOCP rewrite (confirmed on a real Windows 11 ARM64 /
    MSVC 2026 host, added as a smoke-test guard).
  * Documentation -- the man page records that fault containment is
    per-OS-thread: xtc_fault_guard_install() must run on every thread
    that hosts a loop (carrier-team request).
  * Full make check clean under AddressSanitizer and
    UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer; amalgamation builds at 1.3.0.

 -- Greg Burd <greg@burd.me>  Mon, 06 Jul 2026 07:29:07 -0400

libxtc (1.2.1-1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Portability + bug-fix release.  Qualified by building and running
    the full test suite on riscv64 (Ubuntu, gcc + clang), x86_64
    (NixOS, gcc), FreeBSD/amd64 (clang), illumos/sparc64 (gcc, big
    endian), and Windows 11 ARM64 (MSVC 2022 and 2026 smoke test) --
    which surfaced several latent portability bugs, all fixed here.
  * Two issues reported by the multithreaded-PostgreSQL carrier team,
    both fixed:
    - Monitor DOWN ambiguity: a monitor registered on an already-dead
      target delivered reason XTC_E_NOTFOUND (-11), which collides with
      -SIGSEGV, so a clean short-lived backend looked like a crash.  It
      now delivers a distinct XTC_DOWN_NOPROC, with a documented
      DOWN-reason contract and an xtc_down_is_noproc() helper so a
      supervisor can tell "already gone" from a real fault.
    - Early-fault containment: a fault before a fiber armed its own
      recovery frame delivered no DOWN.  xtc_proc now auto-arms a
      default recovery frame at proc entry, so ANY contained fault
      delivers a DOWN (reason = the signal number).  A stack-overflow
      fault still escalates (the guard page is gone), and a
      critical-section fault still aborts -- PANIC semantics preserved.
  * Portability fixes: gate the involuntary-preemption ucontext redirect
    on __linux__ (glibc-only gregs/REG_RIP) so FreeBSD/illumos build;
    link -lrt for timer_create where the POSIX timers live in librt;
    xtc_preempt_supported() is now a runtime probe (some FreeBSD hosts
    lack a per-thread CPU-time timer); portable [[:space:]] in s_cfg
    (BSD/illumos sed has no \s); export CC to the shell tests (illumos
    has gcc but no cc); link os_alloc + a no-op unsafe-bracket stub into
    the minimal meson M0 library; exclude that stub from the MSVC build.
  * Also confirms the intended public deallocator: xtc_free() (v1.2.0)
    is the stable free for all "free with xtc_free" buffers; the older
    __os_free references in comments were corrected.
  * DST sim suite grows to 41 tests (added a monitored N-loop clean-
    teardown test that reproduced the DOWN-ambiguity diagnosis).  Full
    make check clean under AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer.

